<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Perusia</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 295 BCE (approx.) · capital Perusia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Etruscan hill city above the upper Tiber, known for its gates, its family tombs and a famous siege by Octavian in 41–40 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Perusia, Etruscan Persna, modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-perugia">Perugia</a>, grew on a group of hills west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>, near the border with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/umbrians">Umbrians</a>. It developed into a city later than the coastal centres, gaining importance from the 6th century BCE and especially in the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE, when its great walls and gates were built.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Perusia controlled river routes and farmland on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> and mixed with Umbrian neighbours. Its aristocratic families buried their dead in chamber tombs with carved urns, the finest being the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hypogeum-of-the-volumni">Hypogeum of the Volumni</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cippus-perusinus">Cippus of Perugia</a>, a stone boundary text of about 130 words, is one of the longest Etruscan inscriptions, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arco-etrusco">Arco Etrusco</a> gate still stands.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Perusia joined other Etruscan cities against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, made truces in 310 and 294 BCE and became a Roman ally after the wars of the early 3rd century. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perusine-war">Perusine War</a> Octavian besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-antonius">Lucius Antonius</a> there in 41–40 BCE; the city was taken and burned. Augustus rebuilt it as Augusta Perusia.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/perusia.jpg" alt="Perusia" width="320" height="429"><figcaption>Image: Abxbay · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Perugia-arco_etrusco.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 295 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Perusia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Etruscan religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Etruscan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Perugia (Etruscan), Persna</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-548&amp;focus=perusia" aria-label="Perusia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4979 647 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Perusia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5299 903L5290 918M5299 903L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5290 918h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5299 903h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5299 903h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-548&amp;focus=perusia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

